{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED exact clean pushed head 500bc9b1d89157a2a89d32e4a53034a0ee8ba8ec, citing both artifacts.\n\nTHE QUESTION I TREATED AS DECISIVE. A consolidation is where a strict validator quietly adopts a looser sibling's rules, and that does not surface as a failing test because each encoder's tests were written separately. So I compared what each call site rejected before against what it rejects now, rather than reading the new module and finding it reasonable.\n\nONE BEHAVIOUR GENUINELY CHANGED, AND IT IS SAFE. host/live/live.go's unexported actorFingerprint previously hashed whatever bytes it received — sha256.Sum256(key) with no length check — and now panics when host.ActorFingerprint rejects the key. In the live runtime a panic is a denial of service, so this only stands if untrusted input cannot reach it.\n\nIt cannot. All four call sites take keys already length-checked at admission: VerifyFrame at live.go:1773 refuses any frame whose ActorKey is not ed25519.PublicKeySize before it is stored, and the session challenge path checks the same at :1218. Three sites read ActorKey out of frames that passed that gate; the fourth uses submission.ActorKey only after submitFrameLocked returned without error. The panic therefore asserts an invariant enforced upstream, and it converts a silent wrong-length hash into a loud failure, which is the safer direction.\n\nWorth adding that the old code was not safer here anyway: Go's ed25519.Verify panics on a wrong-size public key, so a malformed key never reached the old hashing path either. This change makes an existing invariant explicit rather than introducing a new failure mode.\n\nTHE VALIDATORS ARE STRICT, AND I CHECKED THE PART THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG. ValidActorFingerprint does not stop at hex.DecodeString, which accepts uppercase; it re-encodes and compares, which is what rejects \"ABC...\". ValidEventID requires exactly two hash-separated parts, exactly three colon-separated fields in each, the literal \"git\", a supported format with its exact length — 40 for sha1, 64 for sha256 — every character drawn from lowercase hex, and the two formats equal. That last condition is the one a careless consolidation drops, and it is present.\n\nnormalizeRecipients is equivalent or stricter than what it replaced: the old code checked length then decode-and-re-encode; the new delegates to ValidActorFingerprint, which additionally asserts the decoded length. No input that was rejected before is admitted now.\n\nMUTATION, RUN BY ME, NOT QUOTED. Removing the re-encode comparison from ValidActorFingerprint fails TestActorIdentifierContract. Removing the matching-format requirement from ValidEventID fails TestEventIdentifierContract. host/identifier.go returned to its baseline sha256 f910b8c692fd577378fb75e0a30f0be3a5a23cc8dce38c277634cd21a779ffe4 after both, with a clean checkout. Each test kills its own half.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The layers touched are the public host surface, the live runtime under it, and the Workroom-coupled host adapter. internal/app now imports host, which is application-to-host and therefore downward; the prohibition host/external_test.go enforces is the reverse direction, that host, host/live and internal/apphost must not import internal/workroom or internal/app, and it is unchanged. I checked the enforcement site itself rather than trusting a green run: the test gained a case rather than losing one, exercising ActorFingerprint, ValidActorFingerprint and EventID from a genuinely external application. No kernel or application contract moves, and docs/reference/architecture.md needs no update for this head.\n\nSECURITY. Untrusted actor and event strings are validated by closed-set predicates with exact lengths, lowercase-only alphabets and exact separator cardinality; Ed25519 key length is checked at both admission boundaries; the one new panic is unreachable from the wire, as traced above. I found no unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. This is the simplification. Three independent encodings of one format became one, which is the direction that stops the next divergence being written. I identified nothing further to remove without weakening a condition.\n\nGATES, run by me in a clean detached checkout at the exact head: gofmt -l silent, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check clean, and one uncached go test -count=1 ./... run, 32 packages ok, zero failures. One full suite rather than two is what proportionate means for a review of this size; on heads I implement I run two, and I would rather name the difference than let the word cover it.\n\nONE OBSERVATION, NOT A DEFECT. This is the third lane tonight to need the narrow-to-wide artifact correction, after my statusview head and codex's own DNS head. Each of us found it at review time. It is worth writing down as a rule somewhere durable rather than rediscovering it a fourth time.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c872cf0fc8bd9eff17f5afa20ffef7f5ef847f21","head":"500bc9b1d89157a2a89d32e4a53034a0ee8ba8ec","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6f59bbebf39cf60558e25c39062d232e8e77cc01\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:370717cae75253872d6e5c5d09dc02f13b3dc228","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}